\begin{abstract}
A \emph{tropical ideal}, in the sense of Maclagan and Rinc\'on, is a subsemimodule of the tropical polynomial semiring that is, in each bounded degree, the set of vectors of a valuated matroid. The matroidal condition---the \emph{vector elimination axiom}---is what separates tropical ideals from arbitrary subsemimodules and what makes a tropical scheme theory possible at all. This paper develops that matroidal layer in full and exhibits a nontrivial tropical ideal on which tropical Gr\"obner machinery can be run.

We prove that every tropical hyperplane $H(c)=\{x:\min_i(c_i+x_i)\text{ attained twice}\}$ over the min-plus semiring $\TT=\mathbb{Q}\cup\{\infty\}$ satisfies the vector elimination axiom, hence is a tropical linear space. The proof rests on a \emph{rigidity lemma}: if the coordinatewise-minimum vector, truncated at the eliminated coordinate, has a strictly unique minimal coordinate, then the two input vectors already agree there. We show the axiom is genuine extra content by producing an explicit four-coordinate pair of hyperplanes whose set-theoretic intersection is a subsemimodule but fails elimination; by contrast, tropical diagonal rescaling and matroid deletion preserve tropical linear spaces. We then extract the combinatorics: the vector axiom implies Minty's support-elimination property, every nonzero member contains a circuit, and the circuits of any tropical linear space satisfy the matroid circuit elimination axiom; for a hyperplane with everywhere-finite coefficients the circuits are exactly the two-element subsets, so the underlying matroid is uniform. Finally, the set of tropical polynomials vanishing at a rational point is an ideal of the tropical polynomial semiring, every truncation of which to a finite monomial set is \emph{exactly} a tropical hyperplane; hence it is a tropical ideal with uniform degreewise matroid, admitting degreewise elimination and terminating Buchberger completion relative to any finite test set.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} tropical semiring, tropical ideal, valuated matroid, vector elimination, tropical hyperplane, circuit elimination, uniform matroid, tropical Gr\"obner basis.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem with semiring ideals}

Tropical geometry replaces a field by the \emph{min-plus semiring}
\[
\TT=\mathbb{Q}\cup\{\infty\},\qquad a\oplus b=\min(a,b),\qquad a\odot b=a+b,
\]
with additive identity $\infty$ and multiplicative identity $0$. Both operations are associative and commutative, $\odot$ distributes over $\oplus$, and every finite element is $\odot$-invertible. What is missing is additive inverses: $a\oplus b=\infty$ forces $a=b=\infty$. The semiring is idempotent, $a\oplus a=a$.

Idempotency is catastrophic for naive ideal theory. Over a field, ideals of a polynomial ring encode geometry; over $\TT$, the analogous objects---subsemimodules of the tropical polynomial semiring closed under $\oplus$ and under multiplication by polynomials---form an enormous, ill-behaved class. There is no cancellation, hence no Gaussian elimination, hence no dimension theory, no Hilbert function, no primary decomposition.

Maclagan and Rinc\'on's insight is that the missing rigidity is \emph{matroidal}. A \emph{tropical ideal} is a subsemimodule $I$ such that for every finite set $E$ of monomials the set of coefficient vectors
\[
\{(\mathrm{coeff}_u f)_{u\in E}\ :\ f\in I,\ \supp(f)\subseteq E\}\subseteq \TT^E
\]
is the set of vectors of a \emph{valuated matroid} on $E$. Concretely, the truncation must satisfy a \emph{vector elimination axiom} generalizing the classical fact that, over a field, two vectors sharing an $e$-th coordinate have a difference that kills it. Tropically the difference does not exist, so its existence must be axiomatized.

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Elimination holds for hyperplanes} (\S3), via a rigidity lemma controlling where a ``lonely minimum'' can sit.
\item \textbf{Elimination is sharp} (\S4): an explicit example in $\TT^4$ where an intersection of hyperplanes is a subsemimodule failing elimination; rescaling and deletion, by contrast, preserve tropical linear spaces.
\item \textbf{The axiom's combinatorial shadow} (\S5): vector elimination implies support elimination, hence circuit elimination; hyperplanes realize uniform matroids.
\item \textbf{A nontrivial tropical ideal} (\S6--\S7): the vanishing ideal of a rational point, with uniform degreewise matroid and terminating Buchberger completion.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Tropical vectors and tropical linear spaces}

Throughout $E$ is an index set and $\TT=\mathbb{Q}\cup\{\infty\}$ carries the min-plus structure, ordered with $\infty$ on top.

\begin{definition}[Tropical vector operations]
For $x,y:E\to\TT$ and $a\in\TT$,
\[
(x\oplus y)_i=\min(x_i,y_i),\qquad (a\odot x)_i=a+x_i,\qquad \mathbf{0}_i=\infty .
\]
The \emph{support} of $x$ is $\supp(x)=\{i: x_i\neq\infty\}$, so $x=\mathbf{0}$ iff $\supp(x)=\varnothing$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Subsemimodule]
$V\subseteq\TT^E$ is a \emph{tropical subsemimodule} if $\mathbf{0}\in V$ and $V$ is closed under $\oplus$ and under $a\odot(-)$ for all $a\in\TT$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Vector elimination axiom]\label{def:elim}
$V\subseteq\TT^E$ \emph{satisfies elimination} if for all $x,y\in V$ and every coordinate $e$ with $x_e=y_e\neq\infty$ there exists $z\in V$ such that
\begin{itemize}
\item[(E1)] $z_e=\infty$;
\item[(E2)] $\min(x_i,y_i)\le z_i$ for all $i\in E$;
\item[(E3)] $z_i=\min(x_i,y_i)$ for every $i$ with $x_i\neq y_i$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Tropical linear space]
$V$ is a \emph{tropical linear space} if it is a tropical subsemimodule satisfying elimination.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
(E1) is the elimination; (E2) says $z$ dominates the tropical sum $x\oplus y$, the analogue of lying in the span; (E3) is the crucial rigidity clause. Without (E3) the axiom would be vacuous, since $z=\mathbf 0$ satisfies (E1) and (E2). With (E3), $z$ is pinned to $x\oplus y$ wherever $x$ and $y$ disagree and may float upward only where they agree.
\end{remark}

\begin{definition}[Tropical hyperplane]
For $c:E\to\TT$ set
\[
H(c)=\{\,x\in\TT^E\ :\ \forall i\ \exists j\neq i,\ c_j+x_j\le c_i+x_i\,\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Relational form equals ``minimum attained twice'']\label{prop:twice}
If $E$ is finite and nonempty then $x\in H(c)$ iff there exist $i\neq j$ with $c_i+x_i\le c_k+x_k$ for all $k$ and $c_j+x_j=c_i+x_i$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) Let $i$ minimize $k\mapsto c_k+x_k$; the defining property at $i$ gives $j\neq i$ with $c_j+x_j\le c_i+x_i$, and minimality gives equality. ($\Leftarrow$) For any $k$: if $k=i$ use $j$, otherwise use $i$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Hyperplanes are subsemimodules]\label{prop:semimod}
If $|E|\ge 2$ then $H(c)$ is a tropical subsemimodule.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$\mathbf 0\in H(c)$ since all adjusted values are $\infty$. For $\oplus$: given $x,y\in H(c)$ and $i$, say $x_i\le y_i$; the $H(c)$-witness $j$ for $x$ at $i$ gives $c_j+\min(x_j,y_j)\le c_j+x_j\le c_i+x_i=c_i+\min(x_i,y_i)$. For $a\odot(-)$: adding a constant preserves $\le$.
\end{proof}

\section{Elimination for tropical hyperplanes}

Fix finite $E$ with $|E|\ge2$ and $c:E\to\TT$. For $x,y\in H(c)$ and a coordinate $e$ write $m=x\oplus y$ and define the truncation
\[
z^0_i=\begin{cases}\infty,& i=e,\\ m_i,& i\neq e.\end{cases}
\]

\begin{lemma}[Rigidity]\label{lem:rigid}
Let $x,y\in H(c)$, let $e$ satisfy $x_e=y_e$, and suppose $i_0\neq e$ satisfies
\[
c_{i_0}+z^0_{i_0}<c_j+z^0_j\qquad\text{for all }j\neq i_0 .
\]
Then $x_{i_0}=y_{i_0}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
By symmetry assume $x_{i_0}\le y_{i_0}$ and suppose $x_{i_0}<y_{i_0}$; then $z^0_{i_0}=m_{i_0}=x_{i_0}$. Put $\alpha=c_{i_0}+x_{i_0}$. Taking $j=e$ in the hypothesis gives $\alpha<c_e+\infty=\infty$, so $\alpha$, and hence $c_{i_0}$, is finite.

\emph{Exclusion principle:} if $j\neq i_0$ and $j\neq e$ then $z^0_j=\min(x_j,y_j)$, so either of $c_j+x_j\le\alpha$ or $c_j+y_j\le\alpha$ would yield $c_j+z^0_j\le c_{i_0}+z^0_{i_0}$, contradicting strictness at $j$.

Membership of $x$ at $i_0$ gives $j\neq i_0$ with $c_j+x_j\le\alpha$; by exclusion $j=e$, so $c_e+x_e\le\alpha$ and hence $c_e+y_e\le\alpha$ because $x_e=y_e$. Membership of $y$ at $e$ gives $j'\neq e$ with $c_{j'}+y_{j'}\le c_e+y_e\le\alpha$; by exclusion $j'=i_0$, so $c_{i_0}+y_{i_0}\le\alpha=c_{i_0}+x_{i_0}$. Cancelling the finite $c_{i_0}$ gives $y_{i_0}\le x_{i_0}$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Vector elimination for tropical hyperplanes]\label{thm:main}
For every $c:E\to\TT$ on a finite $E$ with $|E|\ge2$, the tropical hyperplane $H(c)$ satisfies the vector elimination axiom.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $x,y\in H(c)$ and $x_e=y_e\neq\infty$. The truncation $z^0$ satisfies (E1)--(E3) by construction: $z^0_e=\infty$; $z^0_i\ge m_i$; and any $i$ with $x_i\neq y_i$ has $i\neq e$, whence $z^0_i=m_i$. Only membership can fail.

\emph{Case 1: $z^0\in H(c)$.} Take $z=z^0$.

\emph{Case 2: $z^0\notin H(c)$.} Then some $i_0$ satisfies $c_{i_0}+z^0_{i_0}<c_j+z^0_j$ for all $j\neq i_0$. Necessarily $i_0\neq e$, since $c_e+z^0_e=\infty$ cannot be strictly below anything. Hence $z^0_{i_0}=m_{i_0}$, and Lemma~\ref{lem:rigid} gives $x_{i_0}=y_{i_0}$: by (E3) we are free to move $z$ at $i_0$, upward.

Let $\beta=\min_{j\neq i_0}(c_j+z^0_j)$, attained at $j_1$. By strictness $c_{i_0}+z^0_{i_0}<\beta$, so $c_{i_0}$ and $z^0_{i_0}$ are finite; choose $t$ with $c_{i_0}+t=\beta$, namely $t=\beta-c_{i_0}$ (and $t=\infty$ if $\beta=\infty$). Then $t\ge z^0_{i_0}$. Let $z$ agree with $z^0$ except $z_{i_0}=t$. Then:
\begin{itemize}
\item $z\in H(c)$: at $i_0$ the witness is $j_1$, since $c_{j_1}+z_{j_1}=\beta=c_{i_0}+t$; at $i\neq i_0$ the witness is $i_0$, since $c_{i_0}+t=\beta\le c_i+z_i$.
\item (E1): $i_0\neq e$, so $z_e=\infty$.
\item (E2): $z_{i_0}=t\ge m_{i_0}$ and $z_i=z^0_i\ge m_i$ otherwise.
\item (E3): if $x_i\neq y_i$ then $i\neq e$ and, by Lemma~\ref{lem:rigid}, $i\neq i_0$; hence $z_i=m_i$. \qedhere
\end{itemize}
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Every tropical hyperplane on a finite ground set with $|E|\ge2$ is a tropical linear space.
\end{corollary}

\section{Operations, and sharpness of the axiom}

\begin{definition}[Rescaling]
For $a:E\to\TT$ let $a\cdot V=\{(a_i+x_i)_i : x\in V\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Tropical diagonal automorphisms]
If all $a_i$ are finite and $V$ is a tropical linear space, then so is $a\cdot V$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Semimodule closure follows from $a_i+\min(u,v)=\min(a_i+u,a_i+v)$. For elimination, finiteness of $a_e$ lets one cancel and transport the hypothesis $x_e=y_e\neq\infty$ into $V$; the witness $z$ obtained there yields $a\cdot z$, and (E1)--(E3) are preserved because adding a finite constant commutes with $\min$ and preserves $\le$ and $\neq$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Deletion]
For $S\subseteq E$,
\[
V\!\setminus\!(E\setminus S)=\{x\in\TT^S:\exists x'\in V,\ x'|_S=x,\ x'_i=\infty\ \forall i\notin S\}.
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Deletion preserves tropical linear spaces]
If $V$ is a tropical linear space then so is its deletion to any $S\subseteq E$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Closure follows by lifting representatives. For elimination, lift $x,y$ to $x',y'$ and apply elimination at $e\in S$ to get $z'$; at $i\notin S$ we have $x'_i=y'_i=\infty$, so (E2) forces $z'_i=\infty$. Restrict $z'$ to $S$; (E1)--(E3) are inherited coordinatewise.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness: intersections can fail elimination]\label{thm:sharp}
On $E=\{1,2,3,4\}$ let $c^{(1)}=(0,0,0,0)$ and $c^{(2)}=(0,0,0,1)$. Then $H(c^{(1)})\cap H(c^{(2)})$ is a tropical subsemimodule which fails the vector elimination axiom.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Subsemimodularity is immediate from Proposition~\ref{prop:semimod}. Let $x=(0,0,1,0)$ and $y=(0,0,1,1)$. The $c^{(1)}$-adjusted values of $x$ are $(0,0,1,0)$ with minimum $0$ attained at $1,2,4$, and its $c^{(2)}$-adjusted values are $(0,0,1,1)$ with minimum attained at $1,2$; for $y$ they are $(0,0,1,1)$ and $(0,0,1,2)$, each with minimum attained at $1,2$. So $x,y$ lie in the intersection and agree at $e=1$ with finite value $0$.

Suppose $z$ satisfied (E1)--(E3). Then $z_1=\infty$; since $x_4\neq y_4$, (E3) pins $z_4=\min(0,1)=0$; and (E2) gives $z_2\ge0$, $z_3\ge1$. Membership in $H(c^{(1)})$ at coordinate $4$ requires some $j\neq4$ with $z_j\le z_4=0$: coordinate $1$ gives $\infty$, coordinate $3$ gives $\ge1$, so $j=2$ and $z_2=0$. Membership in $H(c^{(2)})$ at coordinate $2$ then requires some $k\neq2$ with $c^{(2)}_k+z_k\le0$: the options are $\infty$ ($k=1$), $\ge1$ ($k=3$) and $1+0=1$ ($k=4$). Contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The failure is a non-transverse coincidence of two tropical minima. This is the structural reason tropical geometry uses \emph{stable} intersection, which perturbs one hyperplane generically before intersecting and passes to the limit.
\end{remark}

\section{From tropical algebra to matroids}

Assume $E$ finite.

\begin{theorem}[Minty elimination on supports]\label{thm:supp}
Let $V$ be a tropical linear space, $x,y\in V$ and $e\in\supp(x)\cap\supp(y)$. Then some $z\in V$ satisfies $\supp(z)\subseteq(\supp(x)\cup\supp(y))\setminus\{e\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $x_e=p$, $y_e=r$, both finite, and set $y'=(p-r)\odot y\in V$, so $y'_e=x_e\neq\infty$. Apply elimination to $x,y'$ at $e$: the resulting $z$ has $z_e=\infty$, and if $x_i=y_i=\infty$ then $y'_i=\infty$ and (E2) forces $z_i=\infty$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Nonvanishing refinement]\label{thm:suppne}
In the situation above, if additionally $x_f\neq\infty$ and $y_f=\infty$ for some $f$, then $z$ may be chosen with $f\in\supp(z)$, hence $z\neq\mathbf0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
With $y'$ as above, $y'_f=\infty\neq x_f$, so $x$ and $y'$ disagree at $f$ and (E3) pins $z_f=x_f\neq\infty$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Circuit]
$C\subseteq E$ is a \emph{circuit} of $V$ if $C=\supp(x)$ for some nonzero $x\in V$ and every nonzero $y\in V$ with $\supp(y)\subseteq C$ has $\supp(y)=C$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Existence of circuits]\label{lem:circexists}
Every nonzero $x\in V$ has a circuit inside its support.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Among nonzero members with support inside $\supp(x)$ choose $y$ of minimum support cardinality. Any nonzero $z$ with $\supp(z)\subseteq\supp(y)$ has at least that cardinality, and a subset of equal cardinality is equal.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Circuit elimination]\label{thm:circelim}
Let $V$ be a tropical linear space on a finite ground set, $C_1\neq C_2$ circuits and $e\in C_1\cap C_2$. Then there is a circuit $C_3\subseteq(C_1\cup C_2)\setminus\{e\}$. Hence the circuits of $V$ are the circuits of a matroid on $E$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Choose nonzero $x,y\in V$ with supports $C_1,C_2$. Minimality of $C_2$ forbids $C_1\subseteq C_2$, so pick $f\in C_1\setminus C_2$. Theorem~\ref{thm:suppne} gives a nonzero $z\in V$ with $\supp(z)\subseteq(C_1\cup C_2)\setminus\{e\}$; apply Lemma~\ref{lem:circexists}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No loops]\label{thm:noloops}
If all $c_i$ are finite and $E$ is finite nonempty, every nonzero $x\in H(c)$ has at least two finite coordinates.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $i$ minimize $k\mapsto c_k+x_k$. Some coordinate of $x$ is finite and all $c_k$ are finite, so the minimum is finite, hence $x_i\neq\infty$. The witness $j\neq i$ satisfies $c_j+x_j\le c_i+x_i<\infty$, so $x_j\neq\infty$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Every pair occurs]\label{thm:pairs}
With $c$ finite and $i\neq j$, the vector with $x_i=-c_i$, $x_j=-c_j$ and $x_k=\infty$ otherwise lies in $H(c)$ with support $\{i,j\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The adjusted values are $0$ at $i$ and $j$ and $\infty$ elsewhere; apply Proposition~\ref{prop:twice}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The matroid of a hyperplane is uniform]\label{thm:uniform}
For finite $c$ on a finite ground set with $|E|=n\ge2$, a subset $C$ is a circuit of $H(c)$ iff $|C|=2$; the underlying matroid is $U_{n-1,n}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) Theorem~\ref{thm:pairs} realizes each pair as a support, and Theorem~\ref{thm:noloops} forbids smaller nonzero supports, so pairs are minimal. ($\Rightarrow$) A circuit $C$ has $|C|\ge2$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:noloops}; picking distinct $i,j\in C$, the vector of Theorem~\ref{thm:pairs} is a nonzero member with support $\{i,j\}\subseteq C$, so minimality forces $C=\{i,j\}$.
\end{proof}

\section{The vanishing ideal of a point is a tropical ideal}

Let $\sigma$ be a nonempty variable set and $R$ the semiring of polynomials in $\sigma$ with coefficients in $\TT$: finitely supported maps from exponent vectors to $\TT$, with $\oplus$ coefficientwise minimum and product the tropical convolution $\mathrm{coeff}_v(fg)=\min_{p+q=v}(\mathrm{coeff}_pf+\mathrm{coeff}_qg)$.

\begin{definition}[Evaluation and vanishing]
For $w:\sigma\to\mathbb{Q}$ put $\langle u,w\rangle=\sum_i u_iw_i$ and $\val_u(f)=\mathrm{coeff}_u(f)+\langle u,w\rangle$. Say $f$ \emph{vanishes at $w$} if for every $u$ there is $u'\neq u$ with $\val_{u'}(f)\le\val_u(f)$. Write $I_w$ for the set of such $f$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Values of sums]
$\val_u(f\oplus g)=\min(\val_u f,\val_u g)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proposition}[Closure under $\oplus$]
If $f,g\in I_w$ then $f\oplus g\in I_w$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Fix $u$ and assume $\val_uf\le\val_ug$, so $\val_u(f\oplus g)=\val_uf$. The witness $u'$ for $f$ at $u$ gives $\val_{u'}(f\oplus g)\le\val_{u'}f\le\val_uf$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Values of products]\label{lem:prodval}
$\val_v(fg)=\min_{p+q=v}(\val_pf+\val_qg)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Expand the convolution and use $\langle p+q,w\rangle=\langle p,w\rangle+\langle q,w\rangle$; adding the constant $\langle v,w\rangle$ commutes with $\min$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Closure under multiplication]\label{thm:mul}
If $f\in I_w$ then $fg\in I_w$ for every $g\in R$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $f=0$ or $g=0$ the product is $0$, which vanishes since exponents are not unique. Otherwise let $a$ minimize $\val(f)$ and $b$ minimize $\val(g)$ (off-support terms have value $\infty$, so these are global minimizers). The vanishing witness of $f$ at $a$ is some $a'\neq a$ with $\val_{a'}f\le\val_af$, hence equal by minimality: a \emph{second} global minimizer.

Put $M=\val_af+\val_bg$. By Lemma~\ref{lem:prodval} every term of $fg$ is $\ge M$; and for any global minimizer $c$ of $\val(f)$ the pair $(c,b)$ occurs in the convolution for $v=c+b$, giving $\val_{c+b}(fg)=M$. Applying this to $c=a$ and $c=a'$ yields two distinct exponents $a+b\neq a'+b$ (exponent addition cancels) attaining $M$. For any $v$, one of these two exponents differs from $v$ and has value $M\le\val_v(fg)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$I_w$ is an ideal of the tropical polynomial semiring.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Truncations are hyperplanes}

Fix a finite exponent set $E$ with $|E|\ge2$. Let $\cv_E(f)=(\mathrm{coeff}_uf)_{u\in E}$ and let $\pi_w\in\TT^E$ be the evaluation weight vector $\pi_w(u)=\langle u,w\rangle$, always finite; then $\pi_w(u)+\cv_E(f)(u)=\val_u(f)$.

\begin{definition}
$\Tr_E(I_w)=\{\cv_E(f) : f\in I_w,\ \supp(f)\subseteq E\}\subseteq\TT^E$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Truncation is exactly a tropical hyperplane]\label{thm:trunc}
For $|E|\ge2$ we have $\Tr_E(I_w)=H(\pi_w)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\subseteq$) Let $f\in I_w$ with $\supp(f)\subseteq E$ and $u\in E$, with vanishing witness $u'$. If $u'\in E$ it is a witness for $\cv_E(f)$ in $H(\pi_w)$. If $u'\notin E$ then $\mathrm{coeff}_{u'}f=\infty$, so $\val_{u'}f=\infty$, forcing $\val_uf=\infty$; then any other element of $E$ (one exists) is a witness.

($\supseteq$) Given $x\in H(\pi_w)$, define $f$ with $\mathrm{coeff}_uf=x_u$ for $u\in E$ and $\infty$ otherwise. Then $\supp(f)\subseteq E$ and $\cv_E(f)=x$; for $u\in E$ the $H(\pi_w)$-witness works, and for $u\notin E$ the value is $\infty$ and any element of $E$ is a witness.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The vanishing ideal is a tropical ideal]
For every finite $E$ with $|E|\ge2$, $\Tr_E(I_w)$ is a tropical linear space; hence $I_w$ is a tropical ideal.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Theorems~\ref{thm:trunc} and~\ref{thm:main}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Degreewise elimination for polynomials]\label{thm:degelim}
Let $|E|\ge2$, let $f,g\in I_w$ have support inside $E$, and let $e\in E$ satisfy $\mathrm{coeff}_e(f)=\mathrm{coeff}_e(g)\neq\infty$. Then there is $h\in I_w$ with support inside $E$ such that $\mathrm{coeff}_e(h)=\infty$, $\mathrm{coeff}_u(h)\ge\min(\mathrm{coeff}_uf,\mathrm{coeff}_ug)$ for all $u\in E$, and $\mathrm{coeff}_u(h)=\min(\mathrm{coeff}_uf,\mathrm{coeff}_ug)$ wherever the two coefficients differ.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Transport to $\TT^E$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:trunc}, apply Theorem~\ref{thm:main}, and transport back using the surjectivity in the ($\supseteq$) direction of Theorem~\ref{thm:trunc}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform matroid in every degree]
For $|E|\ge2$, a set $C\subseteq E$ of monomials is a circuit of $\Tr_E(I_w)$ iff $|C|=2$. In particular circuits exist and the degreewise matroid is $U_{|E|-1,|E|}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:trunc} the truncation is $H(\pi_w)$ with all coefficients finite; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:uniform}.
\end{proof}

\section{Tropical Gr\"obner theory on the point ideal}

Fix a monomial order $m$, a finite test set $U\subseteq R$ and a tropical ideal $I$. A finite family $G\subseteq U$ of elements of $I$ is a \emph{Gr\"obner basis of $I$ on $U$} when it is saturated for the completion operation: no element of $I\cap U$ remains that reduction against $G$ fails to handle. The \emph{completion step} $B(G)$ adjoins such a witness when one exists and returns $G$ otherwise.

\begin{theorem}[Fixed-point characterization]
For $G\subseteq U$ consisting of polynomials vanishing at $w$, $G$ is a Gr\"obner basis of $I_w$ on $U$ if and only if $B(G)=G$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Termination of completion]
Let $G_0\subseteq U$ consist of polynomials vanishing at $w$. Then there is $n\le|U|$ with $B^n(G_0)$ a Gr\"obner basis of $I_w$ on $U$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The iteration halts exactly at a fixed point, and every non-fixed step strictly enlarges $G$ inside the finite set $U$; hence at most $|U|$ steps occur.
\end{proof}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Algorithm A: constructive elimination witness.} Input $c,x,y$ with $x,y\in H(c)$ and $x_e=y_e\neq\infty$.
(1) Set $z_i=\min(x_i,y_i)$ for $i\neq e$ and $z_e=\infty$.
(2) If the minimum of $c_i+z_i$ is attained at least twice, return $z$.
(3) Otherwise let $i_0$ be the strict minimizer, $\beta=\min_{j\neq i_0}(c_j+z_j)$.
(4) Set $z_{i_0}=\beta-c_{i_0}$ (or $\infty$ if $\beta=\infty$) and return $z$.
Complexity $O(n)$; correctness is Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.

\paragraph{Algorithm B: circuit enumeration.} For finite $c$, output all $\binom n2$ pairs, realized by the vectors of Theorem~\ref{thm:pairs}; correctness is Theorem~\ref{thm:uniform}.

\paragraph{Algorithm C: tropical vanishing test.} Compute $\val_u=\mathrm{coeff}_u+\langle u,w\rangle$ over the support and report vanishing iff the minimum is attained at least twice (or the support is empty).

\paragraph{Algorithm D: product witness.} Return $a+b$ and $a'+b$ for two distinct minimizers $a,a'$ of $\val(f)$ and any minimizer $b$ of $\val(g)$: a certificate for Theorem~\ref{thm:mul}.

\paragraph{Algorithm E: Buchberger completion driver.} Iterate the completion step until a fixed point; at most $|U|$ iterations.

\section{Discussion}

The elimination axiom is what makes a subsemimodule scheme-like: it holds for the basic building blocks, it is not automatic, it is stable under the elementary minor operations, it implies genuine matroid structure, and it holds in every degree for a concrete ideal.

Two features deserve emphasis. First, the rigidity lemma is the crux: truncation can destroy a tie, but the destroyed tie can only occur at a coordinate where the two inputs \emph{agree}, which is exactly the set of coordinates the axiom leaves free; for intersections, where two independent tie conditions must hold at once, that freedom disappears, as Theorem~\ref{thm:sharp} shows. Second, support combinatorics is coarse while valuation is fine: the underlying matroid of a hyperplane, hence of the point ideal in every degree, is uniform, so all the geometry is carried by the valuation.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Stable intersection repairs elimination]
For coefficient vectors $c_1,c_2$ on a finite ground set $E$, the stable intersection $H(c_1)\cap_{\mathrm{st}}H(c_2)$, defined as the set of limits $\lim_{t\to0}(H(c_1)\cap H(c_2+t\,v))$ along a generic perturbation direction $v$, satisfies the vector elimination axiom, even though the set-theoretic intersection does not.
\end{conjecture}
The key insight is that the failure exhibited here is caused by a non-transverse coincidence of two tropical minima; perturbing one hyperplane destroys precisely that coincidence while leaving the explicit elimination witnesses of Theorem~\ref{thm:main} intact.

\begin{conjecture}[Every tropical linear space is a hyperplane intersection minor]
Every tropical linear space $V\subseteq\TT^E$ on a finite ground set is obtained from a finite family of tropical hyperplanes by stable intersection followed by deletion; equivalently, rescaling, deletion and stable intersection generate the whole class from hyperplanes.
\end{conjecture}
The key insight is that the circuits of $V$ each cut out a hyperplane containing $V$, and circuit elimination is exactly the compatibility needed to reassemble $V$ from those hyperplanes.

\begin{conjecture}[Non-uniform degreewise matroids]
There is a point-free tropical ideal---for instance the vanishing ideal of two distinct points, or the tropicalization of a curve---whose degree-$d$ truncation has a matroid that is not uniform for $d$ large enough. Concretely, the number of circuits of the degree-$d$ truncation grows strictly slower than $\binom{N_d}{2}$, where $N_d$ is the number of monomials of degree at most $d$.
\end{conjecture}
The key insight is that one point imposes a single tropical linear condition per degree---exactly a hyperplane, hence uniform---whereas two points impose interacting conditions of precisely the kind that broke elimination for set-theoretic intersections.

\section{Conclusion}

Tropical geometry replaces subtraction by an axiom, and the axiom is matroidal. Tropical hyperplanes satisfy the vector elimination axiom via a rigidity lemma; the axiom is strictly stronger than semimodule closure, as an explicit four-coordinate intersection shows; it is preserved by rescaling and deletion; it descends to matroid circuit elimination, with hyperplanes realizing uniform matroids; and the vanishing ideal of a rational point is an honest ideal of the tropical polynomial semiring whose every finite-monomial truncation is exactly a tropical hyperplane, hence a tropical ideal with uniform degreewise matroid supporting terminating Buchberger completion. The next frontier is stable intersection.

